  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/dcf326a7962c01814c937e07083193a107597e1995fb66dffd5776a40cf07a9f.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $F?$",
  "worked_solution": "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/189c1f77e7d2dfd97f51e05f4be55423d100921d2dcad5cafe4be4d90463e8d8.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${F}^{\u2032}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${F}^{\u2032}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=-1$ because $F$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $F$ are $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{5},-1).$",
